-- title:
--   In a rational space of dimension at most five with no degree-one triple, every configuration has a ratio of degree exactly two
-- statement:
--   Let $u \in \mathbb{C}$ be transcendental, that is, $u \notin \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the algebraic numbers); spans and linear independence are over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $W \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ be a finite-dimensional $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-vector subspace of dimension at most $5$ with $1, u, u^{-1} \in W$, every element of $W$ lying in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(u)$, the subfield of $\mathbb{C}$ generated by $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $u$. Suppose that (D1) fails for $W$: there are no linearly independent $t_0, t_1, t_2$ with $t_j, ut_j \in W$. Let $x_0, x_1 \in \mathbb{C}$ be linearly independent and $y_0, y_1, y_2 \in \mathbb{C}$ linearly independent, with $x_iy_j \in W$ for all $i, j$ (a $2 \times 3$ configuration in $W$). Then $x_1q(u) = x_0p(u)$ for some coprime $p, q \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}[X]$ with $\max(\deg p, \deg q) = 2$.
--
--   So in such a space every configuration has a ratio $x_1/x_0 = p(u)/q(u)$ of degree exactly $2$.
--
--   **Proof.** `DiazModulus.rational_space_configuration_coprime_ratio` (10.141) writes $x_1/x_0 = p(u)/q(u)$ with $p, q$ coprime and $\max(\deg p, \deg q) \geq 1$; `DiazModulus.two_by_three_configuration_ratio_degree_le_two` (10.88) bounds the degree by $2$; and degree $1$ is excluded by `DiazModulus.mobius_ratio_configuration_u_multiplier_triple` (10.142), which would give a linearly independent triple $t$ with $t_j, ut_j \in W$, against the hypothesis.
--
--   **Novelty.** None claimed; routine. A combination of `DiazModulus.rational_space_configuration_coprime_ratio` (10.141), `DiazModulus.two_by_three_configuration_ratio_degree_le_two` (10.88) and `DiazModulus.mobius_ratio_configuration_u_multiplier_triple` (10.142).
-- source:
